<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!--
Starts a mission from the GliderDos prompt, then falls out so the next
script in the chain takes over.

Written to sit in front of riot.xml, which begins by waiting for a
surfacing and so cannot start the mission it then shepherds:

    sfmc-xml-engine start_mission.xml riot.xml <glider flags> <send flag>

This is an ordinary SFMC script.  Chaining happens in the runner, not
in the XML, so nothing here relies on a language feature SFMC does not
have.

Change the mission name below to suit.  It is riot_sim.mi here because
that is what get-newest-mission-status reports for osusim.
-->

<gliderScript>

    <!--
    An empty matchExpression fires on entry, so the command goes out as
    soon as the script starts, without waiting for the glider to say
    anything first.
    -->
    <initialState name="startMission">
        <transitions>
            <transition matchExpression="" toState="verifyStarted">
                <action type="glider" command="run riot_sim.mi" />
            </transition>
        </transitions>
    </initialState>

    <!--
    Wait for evidence the mission is actually running before handing
    over.  "LOG FILE OPENED" is glider output at mission start, and is
    matched the same way by vacuum_test_send_data_2hrs.xml.

    Deliberately NOT matching the mission name: the dockserver echoes
    submitted commands, so a pattern like /riot_sim/ would match the
    echo of our own "run" and prove nothing.

    The timer is a fallback, not the happy path.  If the confirmation
    is missed the chain still advances rather than stranding here, and
    riot.xml simply waits for the surfacing as it always does.  The
    value is 2 MINUTES, because the timeout attribute is in minutes.
    -->
    <state name="verifyStarted">
        <transitions>
            <transition matchExpression="LOG FILE OPENED" toState="handoff" />
            <transition timeout="2" toState="handoff" />
        </transitions>
    </state>

    <!-- Reaching a final state is what hands control to the next script. -->
    <finalState name="handoff">
    </finalState>

</gliderScript>
